  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 233 PM CDT THU JUL 30 2026\n\nFocus of convection this afternoon is near a stalled out frontal\nboundary in the southern portion of the forecast area. That will\nremain the case over the next 18 to 24 hours.\n\nThat front is forecast to push back northward during the day on\nFriday, in advance of an upper level shortwave trough forecast to\nto dig into the eastern states. PWATs and instability will also\nstart to increase by late Friday. The upper level trough and \nassociated surface low will provide a substantial amount of lift\non Saturday, much more than we typically see in this time of year.\nThis will lead to widespread showers and thunderstorms. Given the\nincreased instability, lowering heights and 500mb temperatures,  \nand the forcing associated with the trough, numerous storms are\nlikely to develop. The main question on potential severity is\nwhether the shear will ample enough to organize storms to a\nsufficient level. Regardless of storm severity, heavy rain will be\na concern as well, especially if the upper low slows down and\nhelps deep tropical moisture continue to feed into our region.\n\nEarly next week, we should transition back to a more typical\nsummertime pattern, with daily diurnally driven convection.\n\n/61/",
